{"skill": "Main Idea", "difficulty": "Medium", "question": "The following text is an excerpt from Theodore Dreiser’s 1900 novel *Sister Carrie*. Carrie, a young woman from a small town, observes certain details of urban life upon arriving in Chicago for the first time.

Cars clanged incessantly, bounded forward, whirled back, and arrived with a din that was deafening. Men and women clambered in and out, hurried, swayed, and gripped their hats in maneuvers that were of the swiftest, and seemingly at all times. The atmosphere was that of a day out of school, but with an underlying sense of it being imperative—this keen, hustling activity in every direction.

