Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": ["A) inhabited", "B) inhabits", "C) will inhabit", "D) is inhabiting"], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of tenses to match the historical context described in the sentence. The past tense verb "inhabited" correctly indicates that the action of humans living in the area occurred in the past, aligning with the mention of archaeological evidence and past climatic conditions.

Choice B is incorrect because the present tense "inhabits" inconsistently conveys the idea of ancient humans living in the area. Choice C is incorrect because the future tense "will inhabit" contradicts the past period described. Choice D is incorrect because the present continuous tense "is inhabiting" also fails to align with the historical context of the past."}

